-- title:
--   Relative degree [ℚ(j)(j_N):ℚ(j)]=ψ(N) at squarefree level
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number which is squarefree. Work inside the field of Laurent series $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\ \mathbb{Q}=\mathbb{Q}((q))$ (Hahn series over $\mathbb{Q}$ with integer exponents). Here `jq` is the element $q^{-1}$ times the image in $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ of the power series `jNum` under the coefficient map $\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Q}$, i.e. the formal $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$, and `jqN N` is its image under `qExpand ℚ N`, the ring homomorphism of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ that multiplies all exponents by $N$, that is the substitution $q\mapsto q^{N}$ giving $j(q^{N})$. Let $K=\mathbb{Q}(\mathtt{jq})$ be the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q}((q))/\mathbb{Q}$ generated by `jq`, and let $L$ be the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q}((q))/K$ generated over $K$ by `jqN N`. The assertion is that the $K$-dimension of $L$ equals `dedekindPsi N`, defined as $\sum_{d\mid N,\ d\ \text{squarefree}} N/d$; for squarefree $N$ this is the usual $\psi(N)=\prod_{p\mid N}(p+1)$. In particular the dimension is finite.
--
--   This is the statement that the modular equation of squarefree level $N$ has degree $\psi(N)$ over $\mathbb{Q}(j)$, equivalently that the modular polynomial $\Phi_N(X,Y)$ is irreducible over $\mathbb{Q}(j)$, formulated for the formal $q$-expansion model of the function field of $X_0(N)$ inside $\mathbb{Q}((q))$. It is used by [`ModularCurve.functionFieldGeneration_of_squarefree`](thm.html#ModularCurve.functionFieldGeneration_of_squarefree), which identifies that function field as $\mathbb{Q}(j,j_N)$.
